        <truefeedback format="html">
          <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>

<p>Let \(X\) be the discrete random variable denoting the number of elephants in one selected \(1 km^2\) region. Then, the mean \(\mu \) is given by</p>

<p>\(\displaystyle \mu = \frac{{@num_elephants@}}{{@num_regions@}} {@disp_a_1@}{@disp_a_2@} \).</p>

<p>The probability if having \(k\) elephants in the selected region is \( \displaystyle P(X=k) = \frac{\mu^k \cdot e^{-\mu}}{k!} = \frac{\left( {@mean@} \right)^k \cdot e^{-{@mean@}}}{k!}\).</p>

<p>Therefore, \( \displaystyle P(X={@num_close@}) = \frac{\left( {@mean@} \right)^{{@num_close@}} \cdot e^{-{@mean@}}}{{@num_close@}!} = {@dispdp(ta_1,2)@} \) (2 d.p.).</p>]]></text>
        </truefeedback>
        <falsescoremode>-</falsescoremode>
        <falsescore>0</falsescore>
