        <truefeedback format="html">
          <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>

<p>The number of expected regions containing \(k\) elephants is given by \(P(X=k) \cdot {@num_regions@}\). This is the same as turning absolute frequencies back to relative frequencies: \(P(X=k)\) represents the expected relative frequency by the law of big numbers, whereas the absolute frequency is obtained multiplying this by the number of regions.</p>

<p>Therefore, the number of expected regions containing one elephant is \( P(X=1) \cdot {@num_regions@} = \frac{\left( {@mean@} \right)^1 \cdot e^{-{@mean@}}}{1!} \cdot {@num_regions@} = {@dispdp(ta_2,2)@}\).</p>]]></text>
        </truefeedback>
